{
 'title': 'How we hear', 'context': 'A lecture in a biology course on the structure of the ear and how hearing can be damaged and restored.',
 'vocab': ['eardrum', 'cochlea', 'hair cell', 'frequency', 'implant'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Good morning, everyone. Today we're turning to hearing, which is in some ways the most mechanical of our senses. I'll begin by tracing what happens to a sound as it travels through the ear, and then we'll look at how hearing is damaged and what can be done about it."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Sound is simply a series of pressure waves travelling through the air. The visible part of the ear, the outer ear, acts as a kind of funnel, collecting these waves and directing them down the ear canal. Its curved shape also changes sounds slightly depending on whether they come from above, below, in front or behind, which helps the brain work out where a sound is coming from."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "At the end of the canal is the eardrum, a thin membrane that vibrates when sound waves hit it. These vibrations are passed on to three tiny bones in the middle ear, often called the hammer, the anvil and the stirrup. The stirrup, by the way, is the smallest bone in the human body, only about three millimetres long. Together, these bones act as a lever, increasing the force of the vibrations. This matters because the inner ear is filled with fluid, and without this amplification most of the sound energy would simply bounce off the surface of the fluid, rather as your voice does when you shout at someone who is under water."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "The inner ear contains the cochlea, which is coiled like a snail's shell. Inside it, running along its length, is a membrane lined with thousands of hair cells. When the fluid moves, the tiny hairs on top of these cells bend, and this bending opens channels in the cells, producing electrical signals. These signals travel along the auditory nerve to the brain, which interprets them as sound."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "What's remarkable is the way the cochlea separates different pitches. The membrane is narrow and stiff at the base of the cochlea and wider and more flexible at the top. High-frequency sounds make the base vibrate most, while low-frequency sounds travel further along and cause the greatest movement near the top. So, in effect, the cochlea works like a piano keyboard rolled into a spiral, with each position responding to a particular note."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Now, the problem. Hair cells are delicate, and in humans and other mammals, once they are destroyed, they don't grow back. The most common causes of damage are ageing and loud noise. Exposure to very loud sounds, for example at concerts or from machinery, can bend or break the hairs, and repeated exposure causes permanent loss. Often the first cells to go are those at the base, so people tend to lose their ability to hear high frequencies first. Many people with noise damage also develop tinnitus, which is a ringing or buzzing sound in the ears that has no external source."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Interestingly, birds are able to regenerate their hair cells, and researchers are studying how they do this, in the hope of eventually triggering the same process in humans. For now, though, the main treatments are hearing aids, which simply make sounds louder, and cochlear implants for people with severe hearing loss."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "A cochlear implant works in a completely different way from a hearing aid. It bypasses the damaged hair cells altogether. A microphone worn behind the ear picks up sound, a processor converts it into a digital code, and this is sent to a thin wire of electrodes that a surgeon has inserted into the cochlea. The electrodes stimulate the auditory nerve directly. One of the pioneers of the modern multi-channel implant was Graeme Clark in Australia, whose team carried out its first implant operation in 1978."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Implants don't restore normal hearing. Users often describe the sound at first as robotic, and the brain has to learn to interpret it, which can take months of practice. But the results can be dramatic, especially in young children, who can learn spoken language if they receive an implant early. Next week, we'll look at balance, which, as you'll see, also depends on the inner ear."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'The outer ear works like a __________, collecting sound waves', 'funnel', 'acts as a kind of funnel', 'It acts like a funnel.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'The stirrup is the smallest __________ in the human body', 'bone', 'is the smallest bone in the human body', 'The stirrup is the smallest bone.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'The eardrum is a thin __________ that vibrates', 'membrane', 'the eardrum, a thin membrane that vibrates', 'The eardrum is a membrane.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'The three middle-ear bones act as a __________ to increase force', 'lever', 'these bones act as a lever', 'They work as a lever.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'The cochlea is shaped like the shell of a __________', 'snail', "coiled like a snail's shell", 'It is coiled like a snail shell.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Bending of hairs produces __________ signals sent along the auditory nerve', 'electrical', 'producing electrical signals', 'Hair cells produce electrical signals.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'High-frequency sounds cause most vibration at the __________ of the cochlea', 'base', 'High-frequency sounds make the base vibrate most', 'High pitches affect the base.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Noise damage is often accompanied by __________, a ringing in the ears', 'tinnitus', 'Many people with noise damage also develop tinnitus', 'Tinnitus is a ringing sound.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Unlike mammals, __________ can regenerate hair cells', 'birds', 'birds are able to regenerate their hair cells', 'Birds can regrow hair cells.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'At first, implant users often say the sound seems __________', 'robotic', 'Users often describe the sound at first as robotic', 'It sounds robotic at first.', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
